(project borge-docs-agent
 (section make
  (doc "Agent-driven implementation — have an agent write code to match spec")
  (status implemented)
  (details
   (doc (|
        borge make is the interactive entry point for agent-driven
        implementation. It opens a pi session with a prompt built from
        the spec, convention rules, existing code context, and the
        declared role. The agent writes code interactively; borge
        commits after (the journal is currently scaffolded — see
        lock-mechanism).

        borge make
          Open pi to implement planned sections. The agent reads the
          full spec, writes code and tests to match planned sections,
          and journals status changes. Auto-commits on clean exit.
          Calls borge balance first to ensure structural soundness.

        borge make spec "description"
          Single-shot spec generation. Not interactive. The agent
          produces a (status planned) section from a description.
          For interactive spec editing, use borge plan instead.

        NOTE on the lockless single-shot path: borge generate code
        (aliased as borge execute) is a SEPARATE single-shot path
        that calls pi -p, prints code to stdout, and does NOT create
        a lock, run integrity checks, or commit. It is distinct from
        the gated make/plan/drift --agent loop above. Use it for
        quick one-off generation; use the gated loop for tracked work.

        borge drift --agent [--fix]
          Open pi to check alignment between spec and code. Read-only
          by default. --fix allows creating journal entries with
          suggestions for spec updates.

        borge commit
          Apply the current lock's journal to .borg files, run fmt
          and balance, git commit. Called automatically after
          borge make/plan unless --no-commit. Can be called
          manually after --no-commit sessions.

        All agent-driven commands create a .borge.lock/ directory
        (see lock-mechanism). The lock is removed after commit or
        abort.
      |))
  )
  (subsection make-command
   (doc "Make code match spec — implement planned sections")
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
         borge make: open pi with the implementer role. The agent
         reads the entire spec, identifies planned sections that are
         ready to implement, writes code and tests. On clean exit:
         borge validates, commits. (The journal is scaffolded but not
         yet written — the commit message is a file list, not a
         journal-derived summary.)
         On error or --no-commit: lock stays, user reviews.
       |))
   )
  )
  (subsection plan-command
   (doc "Edit the spec — add sections, refine descriptions")
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
         borge plan: open pi with the spec-writer role. The agent
         reads the spec, identifies gaps, adds sections, refines
         descriptions, updates statuses. Journals all changes.
       |))
   )
  )
  (subsection drift-agent-command
   (doc "Check and optionally fix alignment between spec and code")
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
         borge drift --agent: open pi with the observer role. The
         agent reads spec and code, reports findings, marks drifted
         sections. No code changes. --fix adds the ability to
         write code repairs and tests. Neither mode may modify
         .borg files except section statuses.
       |))
   )
  )
  (subsection answer-command
   (doc "Answer ask comments in .borg files")
   (status planned)
   (details
    (doc (|
         borge answer: Scan .borg files for open ask comments and
         generate responses. An open ask is a (* author ask (|...|) *)
         comment without a corresponding (* agent response (|...|) *)
         or with an empty response slot.

         Usage:
           borge answer                    # Answer asks in current file
           borge answer --all              # Answer asks in all .borg files
           borge answer --file path.borg   # Answer asks in specific file
           borge answer --dry-run          # Show what would be answered

         Process:
         1. Parse specified .borg file(s) for ask comments
         2. Filter to asks without responses (or empty responses)
         3. For each open ask:
            - Build context from surrounding section
            - Query LLM with ask content + context
            - Insert (* agent response (|answer|) *) inline
         4. Journal all responses (role: analyst)

         Context building:
         - Parent section name and status
         - Sibling subsections (names only)
         - Preceding doc paragraphs (max 200 tokens)
         - The ask comment itself

         What is NOT included:
         - Full implementation code
         - Other .borg files (unless --all)
         - Historical responses or changelog

         Exit codes:
         - 0: All asks answered (or no asks found)
         - 1: LLM error or timeout
         - 2: Parse error in .borg file
         - 3: User abort (--no-commit mode)
       |))
   )
  )
  (subsection context-management
   (doc "Minimal context to respect token limits")
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
         The prompt sent to pi is intentionally minimal to avoid
         exhausting the agent's context window (~20K tokens consumed
         in seconds with full spec).

         Context structure by role:

         borge make (implementer)
           - Planned sections: name + one-line description (backlog)
           - Implemented sections: names only as guardrails
           - Existing module names: flat list
           - Max ~500 tokens total

         borge plan (spec-writer)
           - All section names with statuses
           - Section nesting structure
           - No full doc bodies in prompt (agent explores files)
           - Max ~800 tokens total

         borge drift --agent (observer/fixer)
           - Planned vs implemented diff
           - Changed module names since last commit
           - Optional: specific section under investigation
           - Max ~600 tokens total

         What is NOT loaded:
         - Full .borg file contents (only summaries)
         - Module export lists (just names)
         - Convention rule details (just convention name)
         - Historical commits or diffs

         The agent uses `read` tool to explore files on demand
         rather than having everything pre-loaded.
       |))
   )
  )
  (subsection loops-module
   (doc "Extract the shared create/launch/teardown loop into a named, navigable module")
   (status planned)
   (details
    (doc (|
         Today the agent loop is duplicated across bin/cmd/Make.ml,
         Plan.ml, and Drift.ml (run_agent) — the identical sequence of
         Lock.create → write prompt → Lock_pi_launch.launch → on exit
         0 Lock.teardown ~commit:true / Lock.remove, on failure keep
         lock. The loop exists but is not named; editing it means
         editing three copies.

         Planned: lift the loop into lib/loop/ as a first-class module.
         Each command (make, plan, drift --agent, and the proposed
         regenerate) becomes a thin wrapper that instantiates and runs
         a named loop. Shared primitives — checkpoint (wait for user
         confirmation), run_to_completion (bounded loop with a
         completion criterion), gate_enforce (mode rules), verify
         (compile/drift/check at boundaries), build_context (per-role
         minimal context), invoke_agent (pi call now, rein phase
         later) — become small composable functions the loops compose.

         A concrete loop is a record of params (role, gate mode,
         completion criterion, verification steps, checkpoint policy,
         context recipe) plus a body composing the primitives. Editing
         a loop edits its params; adding a loop instantiates the
         template.

         This is future work that leans on rein: when borge migrates
         from pi to rein, these loop primitives map onto rein's effects
         (call_llm, call_tool, checkpoint, spawn_phase, telemetry,
         yield_artifact) with minimal rewrite. Building the module now
         against pi makes that migration a primitive swap, not a
         restructure. See future-work for the migration note.
       |))
   )
  )
 )
 (section execute
  (doc "Make code match spec — interactive agent mode with role-gated behavior")
  (status implemented)
  (* agent note (|
     roerick, on the envisioned workflow (2026-05-17): "I'm kind of
     envisioning a workflow where we go like, okay, generate the code
     based on the spec. Okay, review the code that was created to
     determine if we've drifted from spec. Okay, fix any drift from
     the spec. Okay, review the code that's created again. And
     hopefully get to something that resembles the code. My hope is
     that we can achieve like a lot of development this way, basically
     by running Ralph loops over with using the spec as a source of
     truth."
   |) *)
  (details
   (doc (|
        Agent-driven commands open pi interactively with a minimal
        prompt built from summarized spec data. The agent's job depends
        on mode: implementer, spec-writer, or observer. Context is
        intentionally limited (see context-management subsection).

        The agent operates in a .borge.lock/ workspace (see lock-
        mechanism). It edits .borg files in .borge.lock/working/ and
        code files in the repo. Commit applies working/ back to repo
        after integrity and balance checks.

        Commands:

        borge make
          Role: implementer
          CAN: write code, write tests, add journal entries (status
               changes, notes)
          CANNOT: modify .borg files (reads from working/, like all
                  agents, but its writes go to code files and journal)
          CANNOT: delete human-authored comments
          INTENT: make code match the spec — planned sections are
                  the backlog, implemented sections are guardrails

        borge plan
          Role: spec-writer
          CAN: modify .borg files in .borge.lock/working/
          CANNOT: write code files
          CANNOT: delete human-authored comments (integrity check
                  blocks commit if violated --force to override)
          INTENT: edit the spec — add sections, refine descriptions,
                  update priorities, reorder

        borge drift --agent [--fix]
          Role: observer (default) or fixer (--fix)
          CAN (observer): create findings as journal entries, add
                          comments to spec, change section status to
                          drifted
          CANNOT (observer): write code files, modify .borg except
                             via journal/status
          CAN (fixer): all observer permissions + write code repairs
                       and tests
          CANNOT (fixer): modify .borg files except via journal/status
          INTENT: check alignment. --fix repairs drift.

        borge commit
          Apply .borge.lock/working/ back to repo, run fmt and
          balance, git commit. Called automatically after make/plan
          unless --no-commit. Blocked by human comment deletion
          (unless --force).

        borge abort
          Discard .borge.lock/. .borg files in repo are untouched.
          Code files may have been modified — use git to undo.

        Why auto-commit? Git is the audit trail. Each session produces
        a commit. Revert if wrong. Review at PR level, not per-change.
      |))
  )
 )
 (section inbox
  (doc "The outlet the gate creates — agent feedback queued for human triage")
  (status planned)
  (details
   (doc (|
        The inbox exists because the gate exists. Borge gates spec
        edits during build turns to stop the failure mode where an
        agent builds X and then rewrites the spec to match X. But a
        gated agent still needs a way to signal that the spec is
        wrong, incomplete, or ambiguous. The inbox is that outlet:
        it turns a forbidden action (edit the spec mid-turn) into a
        queued one (file a concern for human review). It is a routing
        layer, not a preservation layer.

        As a consequence of being the gate's outlet, the inbox also
        absorbs the old issue-tracker section. Everything that needs
        human attention before it becomes a spec change goes through
        the inbox — bugs, drift findings, scope concerns, TODOs,
        external reports. One queue, one format, one lifecycle.

        Why the inbox replaced the old bug tracker:
        The old .borge-bug format and the inbox concept were the
        same shape — both are "something that needs review before
        it affects the spec." Keeping them separate meant two file
        formats, two lifecycles, two sets of commands. A bug
        report from a chatbot and a scope concern from an agent
        are both inbox items that need triage.

        The inbox also enforces spec-first discipline at the tool
        level — not just by convention. The current borge discipline
        says "no unspec'd code," but nothing prevents an agent from
        quietly editing a .borg file during implementation. The
        inbox fixes this:

        - Agents in Plan mode CAN edit .borg files directly
        - Agents in any other mode CANNOT edit .borg files
        - Instead they file inbox items requesting spec changes
        - Inbox items require human review before becoming spec
        - This prevents unseen scope drift

        Five sources of inbox items:

        1. AGENT-FILED SCOPE CONCERNS. During implementation,
           if an agent discovers the spec is wrong or incomplete,
           it does NOT edit the spec. It files an inbox item
           describing the needed change and why. This forces the
           scope change to be visible and reviewed. (This replaces
           the old architectural-decisions subsection — same
           concept, now part of a unified queue.)

        2. BUG REPORTS. From agents, users, or chatbots. A bug
           is an inbox item where something is broken. Drift
           findings from borge drift automatically create bug-type
           inbox items. Semantic review findings (from borge review)
           also create bug-type items.

        3. TODO AGGREGATION. All TODO markers across .borg files
           and .ml files are surfaced as inbox items. A TODO in
           code is an implicit spec gap — it says "I didn't
           implement what was planned" or "something was planned
           but not yet spec'd." Either way it needs triage.

        4. EXTERNAL FILINGS. Users and chatbots can file inbox
           items directly — bug reports, scope concerns, feature
           requests, "this was implemented differently than I
           expected." These are the same shape as agent-filed
           items but come from outside the pipeline.

        5. DRIFT AND REVIEW FINDINGS. borge drift and borge review
           produce findings automatically. These are linked to
           inbox items instead of being a parallel tracking system.

        Inbox item types (source field):
        - scope-concern: spec is wrong, incomplete, or ambiguous
        - bug-report: something is broken
        - todo-aggregation: surfaced TODO marker
        - feature-request: user or chatbot wants something new
        - implementation-divergence: code doesn't match spec
        - drift-finding: from borge drift --agent
        - review-finding: from borge review (semantic or doc)

        Lifecycle:
        triage → acknowledged → plan-approved → spec-updated → closed

        The critical transition is plan-approved → spec-updated.
        Only Plan mode can make it. Only after human review.
        This is what prevents scope drift.

        Other resolutions:
        - wontfix: acknowledged but not acting on it
        - duplicate: same as an existing item
        - fixed-in-code: bug fixed without spec change
      |))
  )
  (subsection inbox-file-format
   (doc "File format for inbox items")
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
         Inbox items are stored as .borge-inbox files in the repo,
         tracked in git alongside code and specs. One queue, one
         format.

         Scope concern example:
         (borge-inbox id:"INBOX-7"
           (title "Need error handling in db_sql")
           (status triage)
           (filed-by agent:implementer)
           (source scope-concern)
           (affects section:"lib.borg:db-sql")
           (created 2026-05-31)
           (doc (|
             During implementation of db_sql.ml, discovered that
             the spec doesn't cover connection retry logic.
             The spec says (status implemented) for db-sql but
             retry was never mentioned. Filing for scope review.
           |))
         )

         Bug report example:
         (borge-inbox id:"INBOX-12"
           (title "parse_expr crashes on nested parens")
           (status triage)
           (filed-by roerick)
           (source bug-report)
           (affects section:"lib.borg:parser")
           (created 2026-05-31)
           (doc (|
             borge parse crashes on the new borge.borg with
             nested verbatim strings. Repro: borge parse borge.borg.
           |))
         )

         Drift-finding example:
         (borge-inbox id:"INBOX-15"
           (title "Doc-code drift in lock_mechanism.ml")
           (status triage)
           (filed-by agent:drift)
           (source drift-finding)
           (affects section:"lib.borg:lock")
           (created 2026-05-31)
           (drift-report-id "DRIFT-2026-05-31-001")
           (doc (|
             Comment says "copies original/ back to the repo"
             but code actually copies from .borge.lock/snapshot/.
           |))
         )

         Fields:
         - id: unique identifier (INBOX-N)
         - title: brief description
         - status: triage | acknowledged | plan-approved | spec-updated | closed
         - filed-by: who filed it (roerick, agent:implementer, agent:drift, chatbot:name)
         - source: scope-concern | bug-report | todo-aggregation | feature-request |
                   implementation-divergence | drift-finding | review-finding
         - affects: which .borg section is impacted
         - created: date
         - doc: full explanation
         - relates-to: optional reference to another INBOX-N
         - drift-report-id: optional link to drift report that spawned this item
         - resolution: optional (when date) (how text) (by who)
      |))
   )
  )
  (subsection inbox-commands
   (doc "CLI commands for inbox management")
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
         borge inbox list [--status triage] [--source bug-report]
           List inbox items, optionally filtered by status and source.

         borge inbox show INBOX-7
           Show full details of an inbox item.

         borge inbox file --title "description" --source scope-concern
           File a new inbox item. Used by agents, users, and chatbots.
           The --source flag determines the type. The --affects flag
           optionally links to a .borg section.

         borge inbox acknowledge INBOX-7
           Mark as acknowledged by human. Moves to acknowledged.

         borge inbox approve INBOX-7
           Approve for spec update. Moves to plan-approved.
           This DOES NOT update the spec yet — it just grants
           permission for Plan mode to incorporate it.

         borge inbox close INBOX-7 [--resolution spec-updated|wontfix|duplicate|fixed-in-code]
           Close an inbox item with resolution.

         borge inbox consolidate
           Aggregate all TODO markers across .borg and .ml
           files into inbox items. Deduplicates against
           existing items. Also surfaces (status planned)
           sections with no details.

         The old commands are subsumed:
         - "borge bug new" → "borge inbox file --source bug-report"
         - "borge bug list" → "borge inbox list --source bug-report"
         - "borge bug show ID" → "borge inbox show ID"
         - "borge bug close ID" → "borge inbox close ID"
         - "borge drift-report" → drift auto-creates inbox items
      |))
   )
  )
  (subsection inbox-gating-rules
   (doc "How borge modes enforce inbox usage")
   (status planned)
   (details
    (doc (|
         The inbox is enforced through mode-specific gating.
         This is hard enforcement at the tool level, not convention.

         In Plan mode:
         - CAN edit .borg files (including plan sections)
         - CAN close inbox items with spec-updated
         - This is the ONLY mode that can modify plan content

         In Execute mode:
         - CANNOT edit .borg files (plan sections are frozen)
         - MUST file inbox items for any spec concerns
         - Can edit code files freely
         - CANNOT edit human-authored comments in any file
           (see comment-protection subsection)

         In Audit/Review mode:
         - CANNOT edit .borg files
         - CAN file inbox items for drift/review findings
         - Drift and review findings auto-create inbox items
         - CANNOT edit human-authored comments in any file

         In BorgEdit mode:
         - CAN edit .borg files (explicit escalation)
         - But changes are tracked and logged
         - Should still file inbox items for major changes
         - CANNOT edit human-authored comments in any file

         The enforcement is at the tool level:
         rein's gate engine rejects edit/write tools targeting
         .borg files when not in Plan or BorgEdit mode.
         rein's gate engine also rejects edits to human-authored
         comments in .ml files (see comment-protection).
         Agents must use the inbox file command instead.
      |))
   )
  )
  (subsection comment-protection
   (doc "Protect human-authored comments in both .borg and .ml files")
   (status planned)
   (details
    (doc (|
         The lock-mechanism already protects human-authored comments
         in .borg files (detecting deletions via diff at commit time).
         This extends the same principle to .ml files.

         What counts as a human-authored comment:
         In .borg files:
         - (* roerick note ... *)
         - (* <name> ask ... *)

         In .ml files:
         - (** ... *) ocamldoc comments (not authored by agents)
         - (* roerick ... *) or (* <human-name> ... *)
         - (*| ... |*) borge-style comments with human authorship

         What does NOT count as human-authored:
         - (* agent note (|...|) *)
         - (* exempt doc *)
         - (* TODO: ... *)  (these get aggregated into inbox)
         - Regular code comments without authorship markers

         Enforcement:
         At the tool level, rein's gate engine inspects edit
         operations on both .borg and .ml files. If the edit
         would modify or delete a human-authored comment, the
         gate BLOCKS it — regardless of mode. The only exception
         is when the human explicitly approves the edit (ASK).

         This is stricter than the .borg-only protection in the
         lock-mechanism. The lock checks at commit time after
         the session. The gate checks BEFORE the edit happens.

         Why: an agent should not be able to silently change what
         a human said in a comment. If the comment is wrong, file
         an inbox item with source scope-concern explaining the
         discrepancy. A human reviews it. The human can then run
         borge normalize to accept the change intentionally.
      |))
   )
  )
  (subsection semantic-review
   (doc "LLM-powered verification that function docs match implementation")
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
         Walks OCaml files, extracts functions and their docstrings,
         uses LLM to verify two things:

         1. Doc-code alignment: Does the comment accurately describe
            what the function does and why it exists?
         2. Internal consistency: Is the function well-constructed,
            or does it have internal contradictions (dead code paths,
            unreachable branches, parameters that are always ignored,
            logic that contradicts the comment)?

         Both checks happen in a single LLM pass per function for
         efficiency. The prompt asks the LLM to evaluate both.

         Process per function:
         1. Extract: function name, signature, docstring, body
         2. Prompt LLM with three questions:
            a) Is there a docstring present? If yes, does it
               accurately describe WHAT the function does and
               WHY it exists? (doc-code alignment)
            b) Are there (status drifted) markers in the
               comment? If so, the drift is acknowledged — flag
               as drifted rather than inaccurate.
            c) Is the function internally consistent? Are there
               dead branches, unused parameters, contradictory
               logic, or structural issues? (internal consistency)
         3. Store result in .borg.meta under node metadata

         What counts as doc-code drift:
         - Comment says "returns Error on invalid input" but the
           function actually raises an exception
         - Comment describes behavior that no longer exists
         - Comment omits important side effects or error cases
         - Comment says WHY but the reason has changed (e.g.,
           "Workaround for bug X" but bug X was fixed)

         What counts as internal inconsistency:
         - Parameter is accepted but never used in the body
         - Match branch is unreachable (covered by earlier branch)
         - Boolean parameter that is always passed the same value
         - Let binding that shadows an outer binding identically
         - Comment says one thing, code does another (this is also
           doc-code drift, but indicates the function itself may
           have been patched without thinking through the design)

         Metadata structure:
         (function name:"parse_expr"
           (doc-present true)
           (doc-status accurate|drifted|missing)
           (doc-accuracy high|medium|low)
           (consistency consistent|questionable|inconsistent)
           (internal-issues "optional description")
           (confidence high|medium|low)
           (checked-at 2025-05-16T10:30:00Z)
         )

         doc-status values:
         - accurate: comment matches implementation
         - drifted: comment has (status drifted) marker OR
           LLM detected significant mismatch
         - missing: no doc comment present

         consistency values:
         - consistent: no internal issues detected
         - questionable: minor issues (unused parameter, etc.)
         - inconsistent: major issues (dead branches, logic
           contradictions, code that can never execute)

         Commands:
         - borge review FILE
         - borge review --dir DIR
         - borge review --all
         - borge review --stale
         - borge review --focus drift
         - borge review --focus consistency

         Findings flow to inbox:
         - Low doc-accuracy → creates inbox item (source: review-finding)
         - Missing doc on exported function → inbox item
         - (status drifted) comment → informational inbox item
         - Internal inconsistency → inbox item (source: review-finding)

         Auto-marking drifted comments:
         When borge review --all detects doc-code drift on a
         function that does NOT have (status drifted), it can
         optionally add the marker:
           borge review --all --mark-drifted
         This inserts (status drifted) into the comment. The
         marker means "a machine checked this and found drift"
         — it is not a fix, but it makes the lie explicit.
         Without --mark-drifted, review only reports findings.
       |))
   )
  )
  (subsection distributed-specs
   (doc "Module-level .borg files with loose function-level coupling")
   (status planned)
   (details
    (doc (|
         Rather than one central borge.borg, each module folder has
         its own .borg file describing that module's exports and
         structure. Function-level detail lives in borg-comments
         within the .ml files themselves.

         This creates a hierarchy:
         - repo/borge.borg: conventions, top-level goals
         - lib/core/lib_core.borg: module exports, dependencies
         - lib/core/spec.ml: functions with borg-comments

         Borg-comment format (OCaml comment containing borg):
         (* (fn extract_functions
              (doc "Pulls functions from code")
              (since "2025-05")) *)

         Drift checking (static/non-agentic):
         Only verifies referential integrity — does a function name
         listed in the module's exports actually exist in the code?
         Does a function in the code appear in the exports?
         No semantic comparison between module spec and function docs.

         Drift checking (agentic):
         The agent reads module spec intent and function comments,
         determines if there's semantic drift, and can file inbox
         items for mismatches it finds.

         This keeps the non-agentic path fast and simple while
         delegating semantic understanding to the LLM when needed.
       |))
   )
  )
  (subsection todo-aggregation
   (doc "Surfacing TODOs as inbox items")
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
         TODOs are spec gaps. They say "I know I need to do
         something here but I haven't spec'd it yet." The inbox
         consolidates them.

         TODO sources:
         - TODO in .borg files:
           (* TODO: handle connection retry *)
         - TODO in .ml files:
           (* TODO: add error handling *)
         - (status planned) sections with no details in .borg
         - Drift findings from borge drift

         borge inbox consolidate scans all sources and creates
         or updates inbox items. Existing items are NOT
         duplicated — the consolidation checks for matches.

         A TODO in code during implementation is NOT a license
         to implement without spec. It's a signal that the
         spec is incomplete. File it, triage it, plan it.
      |))
   )
  )
 )
 (section review
  (doc "Review code quality against spec using LLM")
  (status implemented)
  (details
   (doc (|
        Every file should carry a quality review — this is the review
        section in each .borg child spec. borge review generates a
        code-quality assessment for files under each spec section.
        The review answers: Does the code match the spec description?
        Are exports documented? Are there dead functions? Is error
        handling present? The review is stored as annotated comments
        in the spec or as a separate .review file.
      |))
  )
  (subsection review-command
   (doc "Generate a per-file code quality review from spec")
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
         borge review FILE: read the spec for the file, read the
         file source, build prompt asking pi to assess:
         - Does every spec described feature exist in code?
         - Are all exports documented? (check .mli, or docstrings)
         - Are there dead functions (unused exports)?
         - Is error handling missing where the spec says 'returns error'?
         - Is the literate description accurate?
         Output: findings as sexp, similar to drift findings.
         These are stored as annotated comments under the file's
         spec section, or in a per-file .review.borg file.
       |))
   )
  )
 )
 (section agent-discipline
  (doc "Rules for AI agents working in borge — operationalizing spec-first development")
  (status implemented)
  (* agent note (|
     roerick, on the design choice that sets borge apart (2026-05-17):
     "Other people are solving this problem by spawning dozens of
     agents and having them work in parallel and at cross purposes.
     But I think we can achieve the same thing with massively fewer
     tokens by simply restricting and gating what the agent is
     allowed to do during a certain implementation phase."

     The rules below are the operational form of that intuition:
     gating what an agent may touch is cheaper than orchestrating a
     swarm, and the spec is the gate.
   |) *)
  (details
   (doc (|
        The literate-programming section declares the philosophy. This
        section declares the operational rules that follow from it.
        These rules apply to AI agents (like pi) and humans alike,
        but AI agents are the primary audience because they are the
        ones most likely to violate them through scope creep.

        Rule 1: No unspec'd code. Every .ml file, every module, every
        significant function must have a corresponding .borg section
        before it is written. If you want to add db_sql.ml, first add
        a (subsection db-sql) to lib.borg with (status planned) and a
        doc block describing what it does. Then implement. This is not
        bureaucracy — it is how borge prevents drift. Code without a
        spec is code without an owner.

        Rule 2: Read the spec before building. Before implementing
        any feature, read the relevant .borg sections — especially
        their (details ...) blocks and (status ...). A (status planned)
        section with detailed doc is a design document: follow it. A
        section marked (status planned) with no details is a blank
        check: ask the human before filling it in. A feature not in
        any .borg file is out of scope — do not build it without
        explicit instruction.

        Rule 3: Follow existing patterns. When the codebase has an
        established pattern (like generate.ml using Agent.run_pi_print
        for LLM-mediated generation), and the spec describes the same
        pattern (like ui.borg's generation-workflow section saying
        "assemble prompt, call the LLM"), follow that pattern. Do not
        invent alternative approaches (like template rendering) unless
        the spec explicitly calls for them.

        Rule 4: Update spec status after implementation. When you
        implement something that was (status planned) in a .borg file,
        update it to (status implemented). When you add a new module,
        add a corresponding subsection. Stale statuses are a form of
        drift — they make borge report inaccurate and mislead the
        next agent about what is done vs. what is pending.

        Rule 5: Check against spec during loops. In iterative
        workflows (Ralph loops, multi-step tasks), periodically
        verify: does my task list match what the .borg files say?
        Am I building something the spec describes, or am I building
        something I invented? If a task item has no corresponding
        .borg section, stop and ask before proceeding.

        Rule 6: Agents write only agent comments. An agent may only
        create, modify, or delete comments where the authorship is
        "agent" or "bot". Agents may NOT create, modify, or delete
        comments attributed to humans ("roerick" or any non-agent
        author). If an agent needs to quote a human, it uses
        (* agent note (|The user said: ...|) *). If a human wants a
        conversation captured in the spec, the human writes
        (* <name> ask ...) and the agent writes (* agent response ...).
        This rule is enforced by the lock integrity check (detects
        inserted and deleted human comments) and by lint (detects
        added human comments via git diff).
      |))
  )
 )
)
